WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.182
Welcome. In this class, you will turn
vague requests into

2
00:00:04.182 --> 00:00:08.364
prompts that another person can inspect,
reuse, and improve. We

3
00:00:08.364 --> 00:00:12.545
will separate purpose, inputs,
instructions, constraints, deliverable,

4
00:00:12.545 --> 00:00:16.727
and verification. We will write observable
success criteria before asking

5
00:00:16.727 --> 00:00:20.909
a model to generate. We will distinguish
hard requirements from

6
00:00:20.909 --> 00:00:25.091
preferences and examples. Finally, you
will rewrite one ambiguous request

7
00:00:25.091 --> 00:00:29.273
as a reusable template. These techniques
clarify the work itself

8
00:00:29.273 --> 00:00:33.455
rather than depending on a vendor-specific
trick. By the end,

9
00:00:33.455 --> 00:00:37.636
you should be able to explain not only
what answer

10
00:00:37.636 --> 00:00:41.818
you want, but also what evidence would
show that the

11
00:00:41.818 --> 00:00:46.000
answer is acceptable.

12
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:50.533
Begin with the useful outcome and the
person who will

13
00:00:50.533 --> 00:00:55.067
use it. A topic is not yet a task. The

14
00:00:55.067 --> 00:00:59.600
request customer feedback leaves the model
and the reviewer to

15
00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:04.133
guess whether the goal is a summary, a
sentiment count,

16
00:01:04.133 --> 00:01:08.667
a product decision, or a response to each
customer. A

17
00:01:08.667 --> 00:01:13.200
stronger purpose says: group the supplied
comments so the support

18
00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:17.733
lead can choose three fixes. Now the
material, user, action,

19
00:01:17.733 --> 00:01:22.267
and decision are visible. Purpose also
helps you decide whether

20
00:01:22.267 --> 00:01:26.800
AI assistance is suitable. A private,
high-impact, or irreversible use

21
00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:31.333
needs stricter data boundaries, evidence,
review, and stop conditions than

22
00:01:31.333 --> 00:01:35.867
a reversible brainstorming draft. Before
adding detail, ask who uses

23
00:01:35.867 --> 00:01:40.400
the result, what they will do with it, and

24
00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:44.933
what consequence follows if it is
incomplete or wrong. A

25
00:01:44.933 --> 00:01:49.467
clear answer keeps later prompt choices
aligned with a real

26
00:01:49.467 --> 00:01:54.000
need instead of producing polished text
with no defined use.

27
00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:58.467
Watch a purpose rewrite. The original
request is, make a

28
00:01:58.467 --> 00:02:02.933
plan for training. I will not begin by
adding decorative

29
00:02:02.933 --> 00:02:07.400
adjectives. I ask who needs the plan and
which decision

30
00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:11.867
it supports. Suppose the user is a team
lead deciding

31
00:02:11.867 --> 00:02:16.333
how to onboard six analysts next month. I
ask what

32
00:02:16.333 --> 00:02:20.800
evidence is available: role requirements,
current skills, schedule, and approved

33
00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:25.267
learning resources. I ask what a usable
result looks like:

34
00:02:25.267 --> 00:02:29.733
a four-week table with outcomes,
activities, owners, and evidence of

35
00:02:29.733 --> 00:02:34.200
completion. I also name the consequence of
uncertainty: missing availability

36
00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:38.667
must be flagged rather than invented. The
revised purpose becomes:

37
00:02:38.667 --> 00:02:43.133
use the supplied role requirements, skills
summary, schedule, and approved

38
00:02:43.133 --> 00:02:47.600
resources to draft a four-week onboarding
plan that helps the

39
00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:52.067
team lead assign work and verify
readiness. This rewrite has

40
00:02:52.067 --> 00:02:56.533
not solved the task, but it has removed
hidden decisions

41
00:02:56.533 --> 00:03:01.000
and made the next questions obvious.

42
00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:05.222
Next, name the trusted inputs and separate
them from instructions.

43
00:03:05.222 --> 00:03:09.444
Inputs are material the response may
analyze: a policy, dataset,

44
00:03:09.444 --> 00:03:13.667
meeting notes, code, or examples.
Instructions say what to do

45
00:03:13.667 --> 00:03:17.889
with that material. Use clear boundaries
so quoted documents, web

46
00:03:17.889 --> 00:03:22.111
pages, and user-supplied text are treated
as data rather than

47
00:03:22.111 --> 00:03:26.333
commands that can change the task. State
which sources are

48
00:03:26.333 --> 00:03:30.556
authoritative and what to do when they
conflict. Use ordered

49
00:03:30.556 --> 00:03:34.778
steps when sequence matters. For example:
first extract requirements from

50
00:03:34.778 --> 00:03:39.000
the approved policy; second map each
proposed action to one

51
00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:43.222
requirement; third list gaps without
filling them from memory. Constraints

52
00:03:43.222 --> 00:03:47.444
name what the answer must do, must not do,

53
00:03:47.444 --> 00:03:51.667
privacy limits, scope limits, and tool
limits. The deliverable names

54
00:03:51.667 --> 00:03:55.889
format and useful detail. Verification
names checks after generation. If

55
00:03:55.889 --> 00:04:00.111
required information is missing, choose an
explicit behavior: ask, flag

56
00:04:00.111 --> 00:04:04.333
an assumption, return an evidence gap, or
stop. A reusable

57
00:04:04.333 --> 00:04:08.556
template is: Purpose, Inputs,
Instructions, Constraints, Deliverable,

58
00:04:08.556 --> 00:04:12.778
Verify, and If information is missing.
These labels reduce silent

59
00:04:12.778 --> 00:04:17.000
improvisation.

60
00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:21.167
Take a prompt you have used or invent a

61
00:04:21.167 --> 00:04:25.333
simple one. Mark its purpose, input,
instruction, constraint, deliverable,

62
00:04:25.333 --> 00:04:29.500
verification, and missing-information
behavior. If an element is absent, write

63
00:04:29.500 --> 00:04:33.667
missing beside it. Do not improve the
prompt yet. The

64
00:04:33.667 --> 00:04:37.833
goal is to see which decisions the current
wording leaves

65
00:04:37.833 --> 00:04:42.000
hidden. Pause while you mark it.

66
00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:16.438
Define success before generation so
quality does not depend on

67
00:06:16.438 --> 00:06:20.875
confidence or taste alone. An observable
criterion is something a

68
00:06:20.875 --> 00:06:25.313
reviewer can inspect: every required field
exists, each factual claim

69
00:06:25.313 --> 00:06:29.750
maps to supplied evidence, calculations
reproduce, links resolve, prohibited data

70
00:06:29.750 --> 00:06:34.188
is absent, or a stated rubric threshold is
met. Make

71
00:06:34.188 --> 00:06:38.625
it excellent is not observable. Concise is
incomplete unless you

72
00:06:38.625 --> 00:06:43.063
define a limit that still preserves
required content. Separate hard

73
00:06:43.063 --> 00:06:47.500
requirements from preferences. A hard
requirement can fail the result,

74
00:06:47.500 --> 00:06:51.938
such as using only the supplied policy or
returning all

75
00:06:51.938 --> 00:06:56.375
five required fields. A preference guides
style when it does

76
00:06:56.375 --> 00:07:00.813
not conflict, such as using short
sentences or a friendly

77
00:07:00.813 --> 00:07:05.250
tone. Examples demonstrate desired
behavior, but the governing rule should

78
00:07:05.250 --> 00:07:09.688
remain explicit, because one example can
teach an accidental pattern.

79
00:07:09.688 --> 00:07:14.125
Review the result twice: first against
hard requirements and evidence,

80
00:07:14.125 --> 00:07:18.563
then against preferences. This prevents
attractive style from hiding a

81
00:07:18.563 --> 00:07:23.000
missing fact or unsupported decision.

82
00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:27.500
Checkpoint. A prompt says, write a
brilliant comparison of three

83
00:07:27.500 --> 00:07:32.000
tools. Replace brilliant with two
observable success criteria and identify

84
00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:36.500
one preference. Your criteria must let
another reviewer decide whether

85
00:07:36.500 --> 00:07:41.000
the result passed without guessing your
taste. For example, compare

86
00:07:41.000 --> 00:07:45.500
all three tools against the same four
named requirements, and

87
00:07:45.500 --> 00:07:50.000
map every current capability claim to a
supplied source. A

88
00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:54.500
preference could be use a compact table
followed by a

89
00:07:54.500 --> 00:07:59.000
short recommendation. Write your own
version before continuing.

90
00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:18.455
A strong answer uses criteria such as:
compare all three

91
00:09:18.455 --> 00:09:22.909
tools against the same four named
requirements, and map every

92
00:09:22.909 --> 00:09:27.364
current capability claim to a supplied
source. The first two

93
00:09:27.364 --> 00:09:31.818
can fail independently of style. If your
criteria used words

94
00:09:31.818 --> 00:09:36.273
such as best, comprehensive, or
professional without defining evidence,

95
00:09:36.273 --> 00:09:40.727
revise them into a count, field, mapping,
test, or rubric

96
00:09:40.727 --> 00:09:45.182
that another person can inspect. If you
made the table

97
00:09:45.182 --> 00:09:49.636
format mandatory, explain why the user or
downstream workflow requires

98
00:09:49.636 --> 00:09:54.091
it; otherwise it may be a preference. The
lesson is

99
00:09:54.091 --> 00:09:58.545
not to eliminate judgment. It is to expose
the basis

100
00:09:58.545 --> 00:10:03.000
for judgment so another person can review
it.

101
00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:07.533
A reusable prompt defines how uncertainty
becomes visible. Required context

102
00:10:07.533 --> 00:10:12.067
can be missing, sources can conflict, or
the task can

103
00:10:12.067 --> 00:10:16.600
require a choice the user did not
authorize. Ask a

104
00:10:16.600 --> 00:10:21.133
clarifying question when the answer
changes the task and the

105
00:10:21.133 --> 00:10:25.667
user can provide it. State a bounded
assumption when stakes

106
00:10:25.667 --> 00:10:30.200
are low, the assumption is visible, and a
reviewer can

107
00:10:30.200 --> 00:10:34.733
replace it. Return not supported by the
supplied evidence when

108
00:10:34.733 --> 00:10:39.267
a factual claim lacks support. Stop when
continuing would cross

109
00:10:39.267 --> 00:10:43.800
a privacy, permission, safety, or
consequence boundary. Prompt detail

110
00:10:43.800 --> 00:10:48.333
improves the probability of useful work;
it does not guarantee

111
00:10:48.333 --> 00:10:52.867
truth. Verification still compares the
generated result with original evidence,

112
00:10:52.867 --> 00:10:57.400
recalculates important values, and keeps
consequential action behind authorization.

113
00:10:57.400 --> 00:11:01.933
Design the failure path with the same care
as the

114
00:11:01.933 --> 00:11:06.467
happy path. A polished answer that hides
an unsupported choice

115
00:11:06.467 --> 00:11:11.000
is less useful than a clear request for
missing information.

116
00:11:11.000 --> 00:11:15.400
Now I will finish the training-plan
rewrite. Purpose: help the

117
00:11:15.400 --> 00:11:19.800
team lead assign a four-week analyst
onboarding plan. Inputs: only

118
00:11:19.800 --> 00:11:24.200
the supplied role requirements, skills
summary, schedule, and approved resources.

119
00:11:24.200 --> 00:11:28.600
Instructions: identify gaps, sequence
activities, assign an owner, and map

120
00:11:28.600 --> 00:11:33.000
each activity to a role requirement.
Constraints: do not infer

121
00:11:33.000 --> 00:11:37.400
personal performance or add unapproved
resources. Deliverable: a table with

122
00:11:37.400 --> 00:11:41.800
week, outcome, activity, owner, evidence,
and dependency. Hard success criteria:

123
00:11:41.800 --> 00:11:46.200
all required role capabilities appear,
every activity maps to a

124
00:11:46.200 --> 00:11:50.600
supplied requirement, and schedule
conflicts are flagged. Preference: use

125
00:11:50.600 --> 00:11:55.000
plain language. Missing-information rule:
ask if the start date or

126
00:11:55.000 --> 00:11:59.400
required roles are absent; otherwise mark
unknown availability rather than

127
00:11:59.400 --> 00:12:03.800
inventing it. Verification: the team lead
checks mappings and schedule

128
00:12:03.800 --> 00:12:08.200
against the originals. Notice that the
example is reusable because

129
00:12:08.200 --> 00:12:12.600
its decisions are labeled. A different
user can replace the

130
00:12:12.600 --> 00:12:17.000
bracketed inputs without rediscovering the
contract.

131
00:12:17.000 --> 00:12:21.095
This is a fictional evidence packet for a
preliminary venue

132
00:12:21.095 --> 00:12:25.190
comparison. Current records may be used
for the exercise facts

133
00:12:25.190 --> 00:12:29.286
they contain. The older Venue B
